    "expectTitle": "What the statute says — 43-10A-17",
    "script": "2. What the statute says (15 min)\n\nOpen — 2 min. Two documents, two jobs. The rules define conduct. The statute defines power. 43-10A-17 was last amended effective July 1, 2022.\n\nMost licensees have read some version of the ethics rules. Almost none have read this.\n\nThe catch-all — 43-10A-17(a)(6) — 4 min\n\nPut the text on screen and let the room read it cold before you say anything.\n\nGrounds for discipline include unprofessional, immoral, unethical, deceptive, or deleterious conduct or practice harmful to the public — and then the operative language:\n\n> …which conduct or practice need not have resulted in actual injury to any person or be directly related to the practice of the specialty…\n\nIt also reaches *any departure from, or the failure to conform to, the minimal standards of acceptable and prevailing practice of the specialty.*\n\nThree things to name:\n\n- No injury is required.\n- The conduct need not involve a client or the practice at all.\n- \"Prevailing practice\" is not defined anywhere in the statute or the rules.\n\nPair it with (a)(8), which reaches violation of any statute, law, or rule of this state, any other state, the board, or the United States — *without regard to whether the violation is criminally punishable* — where the licensee knows or should know.\n\nAnd (a)(5), under which discipline by any other lawful licensing authority is independently disciplinable here. Relevant to anyone practicing under compact privilege.\n\nPrivilege does not hold — 4 min\n\nTwo provisions, back to back. This is the segment that lands hardest.\n\n43-10A-17(a)(10) and (c). If the board has reasonable basis to believe a licensee is practicing while incapacitated by substance abuse or mental or physical illness, it may require submission to examination, and may obtain the licensee's *personal psychiatric, psychological, and mental health records.* Those results and records are admissible in any hearing before the board, *notwithstanding any claim of privilege under a contrary rule of law or statute.* Failure to submit or produce permits summary suspension where public health, safety, and welfare imperatively require it.\n\n43-10A-17(h)(3). Where a licensee is the subject of a board inquiry, records relating to any person who received services from that licensee are admissible *regardless of any statutory privilege* — reviewed in camera and not disclosed to the public.\n\nSay it plainly: your own treatment record and your clients' records both become evidence.\n\nThree practical traps — 4 min\n\nSurrender is revocation. Under (l), voluntary surrender of a license, or failure to renew by the end of an established penalty period, has the same effect as a revocation, subject to reinstatement only in the board's discretion. Stepping away quietly is not an exit.\n\nService to your last address of record. Under (k), notice goes by certified mail or statutory overnight delivery to the last known address on file. If it returns undeliverable and the licensee cannot be located after diligent effort, the division director is deemed the agent for service. Move without updating the board and discipline can proceed without your ever receiving notice.\n\nThe fine is $500. Under (d)(7), the maximum monetary penalty is five hundred dollars per violation. Say it out loud and let it sit. The money was never the penalty.\n\nClose the segment — 1 min\n\nSanctions under (d) run from refusal to renew, through public or private reprimand, suspension, limitation, and revocation, to conditioning the penalty on the licensee's submission to care or treatment. Under (e) the board may withhold judgment or impose it and suspend enforcement on probation.\n\nNote that under (j) a private reprimand is not a contested case — no notice or hearing is required, though the licensee may appear on request. Judicial review under (f) lies solely in the superior court of the county of the board's domicile."
