    "expectTitle": "Case A — the record that doesn't separate",
    "script": "3. Case A — The record that doesn't separate (25 min)\n\nFacts\n\nMarcus and Dana W. began couples therapy with you fourteen months ago. Married eleven years, one child, age seven. You conceptualized and documented the couple as the treatment unit. Both signed your couples informed consent, which included a no-secrets provision.\n\nNine months in, Dana disclosed during a conjoint session that she had ended an affair two years prior. Marcus already knew. Your notes from that period document both partners' processing of it.\n\nFour months ago, Marcus disclosed during a conjoint session that he had been arrested for DUI at twenty-two and had never told Dana. That session is documented.\n\nTherapy ended six weeks ago when Dana filed for divorce. Custody is contested.\n\nYou receive two things. First, a signed release from Marcus authorizing disclosure of \"all treatment records\" to his attorney. Two weeks later, a subpoena *duces tecum* from Dana's attorney for the complete file.\n\nNeither is a court order.\n\nThe rules in tension\n\n| Provision | What it requires |\n|---|---|\n| 135-7-.03(2)(a)(2) | May reveal information regarding only those clients who have consented. Marcus consented. Dana did not. |\n| 135-7-.03(2)(c) | When providing any client access to that client's records, failing to protect the confidences of other persons contained in that record is unprofessional conduct. |\n| 135-7-.01(2)(m) | Failing to provide information regarding a client's evaluation or treatment, in a timely fashion and to the extent deemed prudent and clinically appropriate, when requested and released by the client, is unprofessional conduct. |\n\nA duty to produce. A duty to withhold. A record in which every note contains both people.\n\nDiscussion questions\n\n1. Who is the client? Does your conceptualization of the couple as the treatment unit determine this, or does the rule? Read 135-7-.03(2)(a)(2) closely before answering.\n2. Marcus's release covers all records. Can you comply with it at all without violating .03(2)(c)?\n3. If you redact, what remains? Does a heavily redacted file mislead a custody court by omission — and is that your problem under this chapter or a different one?\n4. The subpoena is not a court order. What does the \"as required by law\" exception at .03(2)(a)(1) actually require of you here, and what does it not?\n5. Dana's affair is in the record, and Marcus already knew about it. Does the fact that it was never a secret between them change whether it is a confidence under the rule?\n6. Suppose you decline both requests entirely. Describe your exposure under .01(2)(m).\n\nFacilitator notes\n\n- Groups reliably split on \"who is the client.\" Push them to notice the rule doesn't ask how you conceptualized treatment — it says *when more than one client has participated in the therapy*, which presumes plural clients regardless of your case formulation.\n- The no-secrets provision governs disclosure between the partners during treatment. It is not Dana's consent to third-party release after termination. Experienced clinicians conflate these constantly.\n- When a group decides redaction solves the problem, ask what a custody evaluator does with a file that is sixty percent black bars — and whether the redactions themselves communicate something.\n- The subpoena/court-order distinction produces the most frequent live error in rooms of seasoned licensees. Many will say they would comply on receipt. Let that surface before correcting it.\n\nSlides: display the three-way tension diagram during the breakout. Hold the subpoena/court-order diagram until after report-out — showing it early gives away question 4."
