    "script": "4. Documentation close (15 min)\n\nThe through-line of the hour.\n\nUnder (a)(6) the board may discipline for conduct causing no injury, unrelated to practice, measured against an undefined standard of prevailing practice. Under (h)(3) and (c), the records that would show what you were thinking are reachable and privilege does not shield them.\n\nWhat follows is not caution. It is that the contemporaneous record of your reasoning is the only artifact that travels with you into a proceeding — and it will be read.\n\nExercise, 6 min individually: For Case A, write the three sentences you would place in the file documenting your decision on Marcus's request.\n\nReport out, 7 min. Take three volunteers, ideally ones who reached different decisions.\n\nListen for and name:\n\n- Records stating the conclusion but not the reasoning\n- Records omitting the provisions that cut against the writer's decision\n- Records written defensively enough to read as anticipating a complaint\n- Records that don't distinguish what was known from what was inferred\n\nClose, 2 min. Two clinicians who decided oppositely can both be defensible. Two who decided identically can differ entirely in whether they are protected. The difference is the record."
