    "script": "Hour 2 — Competing obligations\n\nPoll mechanics\n\nSet every poll to anonymous, results shown immediately after close.\n\nDo not display the previous poll's results next to the current one. People anchor to the prior number and the movement flattens. Show each result alone, in the moment. Reveal the full trend line only at the close, when it lands as a reveal instead of a nudge.\n\nKeep the primary question identical at every rung. Changing the wording invalidates the comparison and the room will notice.\n\nPrimary poll, repeated verbatim at each rung:\n\n> Do you disclose now? — Yes / No\n\nThat's it. No \"maybe,\" no \"it depends,\" no \"need more information.\" The discomfort of being forced to choose is the mechanism. When someone protests that they'd need more information, that's the correct clinical instinct and also the exact position a licensee is in at the moment of decision. Say so and make them pick."
