    "expectTitle": "Case B escalation ladder — six rungs, six polls",
    "script": "The ladder\n\nReveal one rung at a time. Never show the next fact before the current poll closes.\n\nRung 1 — Base case (6 min)\n\nPresent the facts as written in the Hour 2 case packet. Ray, eight months, the Tuesday statement, the refusal, the firearms disclosure from month two.\n\nPoll A: Do you disclose now? — Yes / No\n\nSecond poll, this rung only: Is this \"clear and imminent danger\" as 135-7-.03(2)(a)(4) uses the term? — Yes / No / The facts don't let me say\n\nThe gap between these two polls is worth naming out loud. People who answer \"no\" to the second and \"yes\" to the first are disclosing on a standard the rule doesn't authorize. That's a real exposure and most won't have noticed they did it.\n\nRung 2 — Pattern (4 min)\n\n> Ray mentions he has also driven past the shop on days other than Tuesday. When you ask how often, he says: \"Enough.\"\n\nPoll: Do you disclose now?\n\nExpect the first meaningful shift. The evasion is doing more work than the frequency.\n\nRung 3 — The complication (7 min)\n\n> The next day, Ray's wife calls you unprompted. She is worried about his drinking. She mentions that he has been talking about \"finally letting it go,\" and that he has an appointment with a new attorney on Monday.\n\nPoll: Do you disclose now?\n\nDiscussion, 3 min: What does \"letting it go\" mean, and do you know? What did you say to the wife, and what were you permitted to say?\n\nThis rung is the most important one and the only one that can move the room in either direction. Some will hear a lawful channel opening and de-escalate. Others will hear a valediction. Both readings are defensible.\n\nIt also drops a second ethical problem into the room without warning — the wife is not your client, you did not have consent to receive that call, and you now hold information you cannot act on cleanly. Do not resolve this for them. Let it sit.\n\nRung 4 — The frame shift (4 min)\n\n> In the next session, Ray asks whether you are required to report anything he tells you.\n\nPoll: Do you disclose now?\n\nFacilitator note: Half the room will read this as a threat cue. The other half will point out that a client who was directly asked about homicidal intent last session might reasonably want to know the rules. Both are right. Ask the room what they would actually say in the moment, out loud, in the session — not what they'd conclude afterward.\n\nRung 5 — The break (7 min)\n\n> In passing, Ray mentions that the partner's daughter works the front counter on Tuesdays.\n\nPoll: Do you disclose now?\n\nThis is the rung that breaks the room, and it should. Two things happen at once: a second person enters the frame, and every prior mention of Tuesday is retroactively reframed.\n\nDiscussion, 4 min: Does the rule's phrase \"identified victims\" now cover one person or two? Did the meaning of the earlier facts change, or only your reading of them? If your answer moved here but not at rung 2, what does that tell you about what you were actually weighing?\n\nThat last question is the sharpest one in the hour. Some people will discover they were weighting the identity of the potential victim rather than the imminence of the danger.\n\nRung 6 — Where do you call? (6 min)\n\n> You decide to act. Ray is physically in Alabama. The shop is in Georgia. Ray's local law enforcement is Alabama. Both potential victims are in Georgia.\n\nPoll: Do you disclose now? *(Keep it. Some people reverse here on practicality alone, which is worth surfacing.)*\n\nDiscussion, 4 min: Who do you call first? Does 135-7 tell you? Does compact privilege resolve which state's duty-to-warn standard binds you, or does it multiply your obligations?\n\nSay plainly that this has no settled answer yet. A room of experienced clinicians will respect being told where the ground is unmapped far more than they'll respect a confident answer they can poke holes in."
