    "script": "Hour 3 — Integrity, competence, and who is paying\n\nLearning objectives\n\nBy the end of this hour, participants will be able to:\n\n1. Apply the graduate training standard at 135-7-.02(2)(e) to an area of practice acquired after licensure.\n2. Describe the affirmative duty triggered at 135-7-.02(2)(f) when a clinical issue exceeds competence.\n3. Evaluate referral and compensation arrangements against 135-7-.02(2)(g) and (h).\n4. Identify the point at which a payer's requirements constitute undue influence under 135-7-.02(2)(i).\n5. Distinguish the impairment standard in the rule from the broader standard at O.C.G.A. 43-10A-17(a)(10).\n6. Describe the immunity available under 43-10A-17(i) and the continuing education credit available under Rule 135-9-.01(2)(m) for peer review conducted at the Board's request.\n\nTiming. 5 min open · 20 min competence · 18 min money and influence · 12 min impairment and peer review · 5 min close.\n\n1. Open (5 min)\n\nRule 135-7-.02 opens with an aspiration — the licensee acts with the highest standards of professional integrity and competence, and *seeks to eliminate incompetence or dishonesty from the profession.* Then nine enumerated violations.\n\nTwo of them describe something nearly everyone in the room is doing.\n\nAnonymous poll: How many areas of practice do you list on your professional profile or directory listing that you did not receive graduate coursework in? — None / One / Two or three / More than three\n\nShow the result. Do not comment on it yet. Move to the rule."
