    "script": "3. Money and influence (18 min)\n\nRapid-fire format. Read each scenario, thirty seconds of table talk, hands up for violation or not, move on. Save discussion for the three flagged below.\n\nThe text\n\n(g) accepting or giving a fee or anything of value for making or receiving a referral\n(h) using an institutional affiliation to solicit clients for the licensee's private practice\n(i) allowing an individual or agency that is paying for the professional services to exert undue influence over the licensee's evaluation or treatment of a client\n\nScenarios\n\n1. A directory service charges you a flat monthly listing fee.\n2. A different directory charges you per lead delivered.\n3. You send a holiday gift basket to the attorney who regularly refers forensic work to you.\n4. Your EHR vendor pays you a credit for every colleague you refer who subscribes.\n5. You work two days a week at a community agency. A client there asks whether they can see you privately instead.\n6. An EAP authorizes six sessions. Your client needs more. The EAP administrator notes that extension requests affect provider panel status.\n7. A school district pays for a student's evaluation and asks that you not include a specific diagnosis in the report.\n8. Your group practice pays you a percentage of what your supervisees bill.\n\nDiscuss (4 min each, items 4, 6, 7)\n\nItem 4 — is a referral credit from a vendor \"anything of value for making a referral\" when the referral is not of a client? Read (g) closely. It says *for making or receiving a referral* without limiting the referral to clients. Most clinicians in these arrangements have never read it that way.\n\nItem 6 — where is the line between a payer setting benefit limits, which is lawful and universal, and exerting undue influence over treatment under (i)? Does it matter whether the pressure is explicit? Does it matter that you cannot prove it?\n\nItem 7 — this is the clearest (i) violation in the set and the one clinicians most often accommodate, because the request usually arrives framed as being about the child's interests.\n\nFacilitator note\n\nItem 2 versus item 1 is the sharpest live distinction. A flat listing fee is advertising. A per-lead fee is compensation tied to a specific referral. Nobody in the room will have thought about which model their directory uses."
