    "expectTitle": "Open — institutional cover",
    "script": "Hour 4 — Supervision, colleagues, and the board\n\nLearning objectives\n\nBy the end of this hour, participants will be able to:\n\n1. Describe the individual accountability standard at 135-7-.04(1) and its effect on the agency-policy defense.\n2. Identify the point at which the affirmative duty at 135-7-.04(2)(d) is triggered by supervisor awareness.\n3. Distinguish the supervisor's duty to the supervisee from any duty to report, including what 43-10A-17(a)(7) adds.\n4. Evaluate the tension between documenting supervisory concerns and maintaining supervisee confidences under 135-7-.04(2)(b).\n\nTiming. 5 min open · 25 min Case D · 20 min agency block · 10 min close.\n\n1. Open (5 min)\n\nMost of Chapter 135-7 concerns the client. Rule .04 concerns everyone else — colleagues, supervisees, and the organizations licensees work inside.\n\nRead the principle statement out loud before showing the enumerated list. The clause that matters is buried in the middle:\n\n> The licensee respects the rights and responsibilities of professional colleagues and, as the employee of an organization, remains accountable as an individual to the ethical principles of the profession.\n\nThat sentence pre-emptively closes the most common defense in the profession. *It was agency policy. My supervisor told me to. That's how we've always done it here.* None of those transfer accountability, and the rule says so in its opening paragraph.\n\nAsk the room how many have done something clinically they were uncomfortable with because it was how their organization operated. Then move on without discussing it. It'll sit."
