    "script": "2. Case D — What you knew and when (25 min)\n\nFacts\n\n> You supervise an APC, eighteen months into her directed experience, working toward full licensure. She is capable, well-liked, and carries a difficult adolescent caseload.\n>\n> In supervision she mentions, unprompted and offhandedly, that a sixteen-year-old client texted her around eleven at night and she texted back for about forty minutes. She frames it as good engagement with a hard-to-reach kid. She adds that this has happened \"a few times.\"\n\nQuestion 1 (3 min): Has 135-7-.04(2)(d) been triggered? The rule reaches conduct that is *unethical, unprofessional, or deleterious.* Which, if any, applies on these facts — and does \"aware\" require you to be certain?\n\nThe text\n\n135-7-.04(2)(d) — unprofessional conduct includes, when a supervisor is aware that a supervisee is engaging in any unethical, unprofessional or deleterious conduct, failing to provide the supervisee with a forthright evaluation and appropriate recommendations regarding such practice.\n\nTwo things to name immediately. The trigger is *awareness*, not investigation or proof. And the duty runs to the supervisee — the rule requires you to tell her, not to tell the board.\n\nEscalation\n\n> One. You raise it directly. She is receptive, acknowledges the boundary problem, agrees to redirect after-hours contact to the crisis line and document any exceptions. You note the conversation in your supervision record in one line.\n\nQuestion 2: Does that one line satisfy \"forthright evaluation and appropriate recommendations\"? What would a board reviewing it two years from now be able to tell about what you knew?\n\n> Two. Two months later you learn from a third source that the texting continued and increased.\n\nQuestion 3: Your duty under (d) triggered a second time. Does it require something different now that the first intervention failed? The rule text doesn't escalate — does anything?\n\n> Three. She is in your group practice. Her sessions bill under your supervision.\n\nQuestion 4: Introduce 135-7-.01(2)(l) — delegating professional responsibilities to a person you know or have reason to know is not qualified. And 43-10A-17(a)(7) — knowingly performing any act which aids, assists, procures, advises, or encourages an unlicensed person to practice unlawfully or outside a disciplinary limitation. Has your exposure changed in kind, or only in degree?\n\n> Four. Her supervision contract ends in three weeks. She has her hours and is applying for full licensure.\n\nQuestion 5: If nothing is documented, and she is licensed, and something surfaces in eighteen months — what does your record show about what you knew? What would you want it to show, and is that the same thing?\n\nThe conflict nobody notices\n\n135-7-.04(2)(b) makes it unprofessional conduct to fail to maintain the confidences shared by colleagues and supervisees in the course of professional relationships.\n\nShe told you this in supervision, in confidence, unprompted.\n\nQuestion 6 (4 min): Documenting protects you and discharges (d). Documenting also records a supervisee's confidence in a file. Where does (b) end and (d) begin?\n\nThen give them the part that changes the calculation: under 43-10A-17(h)(1), a person conducting a board investigation *shall have access to and may examine any writing, document, or other material relating to the fitness of any licensee* — with subpoena power to compel it. Supervision records are reachable. There is no version of this where your notes are private.\n\nFacilitator notes\n\n- Expect the room to split on whether the first disclosure triggers (d) at all. Some will call it a supervision issue rather than an ethics issue. Ask them what the difference is under the rule text — the rule doesn't recognize that distinction.\n- The most common wrong answer to Question 3 is \"report her.\" The rule doesn't say that. Let the room find that the duty as written is narrower than their instinct, and then sit with whether the narrow duty is sufficient.\n- Question 6 is the sharpest one. Supervisors have almost universally never thought about their supervision notes as discoverable, and they are.\n- Do not resolve the (b)/(d) tension. Name it as a genuine conflict in the chapter and move on."
