    "expectTitle": "Advertising: find it, fix it, turn it on themselves",
    "script": "2. The advertising hunt (31 min)\n\nThe text\n\n135-7-.07(1). The licensee adheres to professional rather than commercial standards when making known their availability. The licensee may provide information that accurately informs the public of the professional services, expertise, and techniques available.\n\n135-7-.07(2). Unprofessional conduct includes, but is not limited to:\n\n- (a) intentionally misrepresenting the licensee's professional competence, education, training, and experience, or knowingly failing to correct any misrepresentations provided by others\n- (b) using as a credential an academic degree in a manner which is intentionally misleading or deceiving to the public\n- (c) intentionally providing information that contains false, inaccurate, misleading, partial, out-of-context, or otherwise deceptive statements about the licensee's professional services, or knowingly failing to correct inaccurate information provided by others\n- (d) making claims or guarantees which promise more than the licensee can realistically provide\n\nRead (a) and (c) twice. Both contain a duty to correct what *other people* say about you. Almost no licensee has ever considered this an obligation.\n\nFormat\n\nThe block runs in three movements: find it, fix it, then turn it on themselves. The competitive opening buys the energy; the second and third movements spend it on something harder than recall.\n\nMovement one — find it (17 min). Four mockups, teams of three to four. Four minutes each on mockups 1 through 3, five on mockup 4. Teams list violations with the subsection cited. Score is violations correctly cited, minus points for false positives — the deduction matters, because otherwise teams shotgun every subsection at everything.\n\nReveal after each. Do not save all four for the end.\n\nMockup specifications\n\nMockup 1 — Directory profile. Build as a therapist directory listing.\n\n- Specialties listed include an approach the bio elsewhere describes only as \"trained in,\" not certified → (a)\n- Name line reads \"Dr. [Name], LPC\" with the credentials block showing a doctorate in an unrelated field → (b)\n- Body copy claims a specific success percentage with no source → (c) and (d)\n- Practice areas include something requiring graduate training the profile doesn't evidence → (a), callback to 135-7-.02(2)(e)\n\nMockup 2 — Practice website, About page.\n\n- Practice name incorporates a protected title with no licensee of that type on staff → Chapter 135-10\n- Staff page lists an unlicensed associate or intern using a protected title → 135-10-.02\n- A results guarantee tied to a session count, with a refund offer → (d)\n\nMockup 3 — Social post.\n\n- A client testimonial, named or clearly identifiable → 135-7-.03(2)(a), and (c)\n- Copy suggesting the clinician can help with essentially anything → (d)\n- A hashtag promising cure or elimination of a condition → (d)\n\nMockup 4 — Content the licensee did not create. This is the one that teaches.\n\n- An old profile from a former practice, still live, listing outdated credentials and a specialty no longer practiced\n- A local news article quoting the clinician as \"Dr.\"\n- An automatically generated business listing with wrong information\n\nAsk the teams to find the violation. Most will say there isn't one, because the licensee didn't write any of it.\n\nThen show them the clause: *knowingly failing to correct any misrepresentations provided by others.* The duty is affirmative and it runs to material you never authored. Old employer pages, aggregator listings, press coverage, automatically generated summaries.\n\nAsk the room, honestly, when they last audited what the internet says about their credentials.\n\nMovement two — fix it (6 min)\n\nFinding the violation is recall. Producing compliant copy that still works is judgment, and this is where rooms fail.\n\nAssign each team one line from mockups 1 through 3 — the success percentage, the results guarantee, the cure hashtag. Four minutes to rewrite it so that it satisfies 135-7-.07(2) and still gives a prospective client a reason to call. Two minutes reading aloud.\n\nMost teams will produce something compliant and inert. Name that when it happens, and put the real question on the table: 135-7-.07(1) requires the licensee to adhere to *professional rather than commercial standards* when making their availability known — in a profession where nearly everyone maintains a directory listing they pay for and a social presence they optimize.\n\nWhere is that line? The rule doesn't say. Ask whether any of them have ever seen it drawn anywhere, by anyone.\n\nMovement three — turn it on themselves (6 min)\n\nNobody finds violations in their own copy.\n\nInstruct the room to pull up their own directory profile or practice website on their phone, right now, and apply (a) through (d) to it. Four minutes silent. Two minutes for anyone willing to name something they found.\n\nExpect a quiet room and some visible discomfort. That is the correct outcome and you should say so — the whole hour has trained them on other people's materials precisely so this moment works.\n\nDo not ask anyone to disclose. Volunteers only.\n\nThe recursive point (2 min)\n\nBoth (a) and (c) turn on *knowingly*.\n\nBefore this hour, a licensee could plausibly say they didn't know a stale listing was out there, or that a duty to correct third-party material attached to them at all.\n\nThey can't say that after this hour. The course itself supplied the knowledge that triggers the duty.\n\nSay this out loud and let it land. It is the most interesting feature of the rule and it makes the hour consequential rather than diverting.\n\nFacilitator notes\n\n- Make the mockups plausible rather than absurd. The teachable ambiguity is in the items people aren't sure about, not the obvious ones.\n- Mockup 4 should score lowest and produce the longest discussion. That's the design working.\n- Expect pushback on \"knowingly\" during movement one. Let it run — it sets up the recursive point at the end of the block.\n- Movement three will produce silence. Do not fill it."
