    "expectTitle": "Assessment — Case E",
    "script": "3. Assessment (14 min)\n\nThe text — 135-7-.05(2)\n\n- (a) failing to provide the client with an orientation to the purpose of testing or the proposed use of results prior to administration\n- (b) failing to consider the specific validity, reliability, and appropriateness of test measures for use in a given situation or with a particular client\n- (c) using unsupervised or inadequately supervised test-taking techniques, such as testing through the mail, unless the test is specifically self-administered or self-scored\n- (d) administering instruments beyond the licensee's competence for scoring and interpretation, or outside the licensee's scope of practice as defined by law\n- (e) failing to make available to the client, upon request, copies of documents in the licensee's possession which have been prepared for and paid for by the client\n\nNote that Chapter 135-12 governs Testing and Assessment separately.\n\nDiscussion 1 — The mail clause (5 min)\n\nSubsection (c) prohibits unsupervised administration, offering *testing through the mail* as its example, with an exception for instruments that are specifically self-administered or self-scored.\n\nThat language predates remote administration entirely.\n\nQuestions: Does emailing a screening measure to a client before a telehealth session fall within the prohibition or the exception? What about a longer instrument administered through a client portal? If your EHR pushes an assessment to a client's phone and scores it automatically, who administered it — and was it supervised?\n\nThere is no settled answer. The value is in the room noticing the rule doesn't contemplate their actual practice.\n\nCase E — The instrument that wasn't built for her (7 min)\n\n> You are retained to conduct a parenting assessment in a contested custody matter. You have administered this instrument perhaps two hundred times and are unquestionably competent to administer and score it.\n>\n> The parent is a recent immigrant, tested in English, which is her second language. The instrument's normative sample does not include her national origin or language group in meaningful numbers. You are aware of this. You administer it anyway, because it is the instrument the referring attorney expects and the one the court has seen before.\n>\n> Her scores fall in a range you would ordinarily flag as concerning. You write the report and note the limitation in a paragraph near the end.\n\nQuestions (5 min breakout):\n\n1. Subsection (b) requires considering validity, reliability, and appropriateness *for use in a given situation or with a particular client.* Did noting the limitation in the report discharge that, or is (b) a duty that attaches before administration rather than after?\n2. Subsection (d) prohibits administering beyond the licensee's competence for scoring and interpretation. You are competent with this instrument generally. Are you competent to interpret it for this client?\n3. Does 135-7-.05(1) — guarding against misuse of assessment results — reach what the court does with your report after you file it?\n\nFacilitator note: This is the only place in Chapter 135-7 where norming populations and instrument fit are addressed, and it is never taught as a cultural competence provision. It is one. Say so.\n\nClose by asking: for the instruments you use most, what population were they normed on, and when did you last check?\n\nNote on (e) — 2 min\n\nDocuments *prepared for and paid for by the client* must be made available on request. Read that alongside 135-7-.01(2)(m) from Hour 1 and 135-7-.03(2)(c) from Case A. Three provisions govern client access to records and they don't fully align."
