    "order": 10,
    "expectTitle": "Research and vendor data",
    "script": "4. Research, and the data you didn't know you shared (12 min)\n\nThe text — 135-7-.06\n\nParticipation in research must be voluntary unless it can be demonstrated that involuntary participation will have no harmful effects and is essential to the investigation. Unprofessional conduct includes failing to consider consequences for participants, failing to protect participants from unwarranted physical and mental harm, failing to ascertain that the consent of the research participant is voluntary and informed, failing to treat research information as confidential, and knowingly reporting distorted, erroneous, or misleading information.\n\nThe move (10 min)\n\nOpen by asking who in the room conducts research. Expect almost no hands.\n\nThen walk the list:\n\n- Does your EHR or practice platform aggregate de-identified clinical data for outcomes research or product development?\n- Do you use a feedback-informed treatment or outcome measurement system? Where does that data go?\n- Have you contributed case material to a training, a book, a podcast, or a presentation?\n- Does any tool in your workflow use client data to improve a model?\n\nQuestion 1: Which of these is research within the meaning of 135-7-.06? The chapter doesn't define the term.\n\nQuestion 2: Your client signed an intake packet incorporating a platform's terms of service. Under (2)(c), has consent been *ascertained as voluntary and informed*? Note the verb — the duty is to ascertain, not to obtain. A signature obtained is not a consent ascertained.\n\nQuestion 3: If the vendor's terms permit a use your client doesn't know about, is the licensee responsible, the vendor, or both? Recall 135-7-.03(2)(d) from Case A — failing to protect the confidences of the client from disclosure by employees, associates, and others whose services are utilized by the licensee.\n\nThat last clause is the answer, and it has been in the chapter since 2000.\n\nClose the block — 2 min\n\nPair with 135-7-.03(2)(e): failing to adequately disguise the identity of a client when using material derived from a counseling relationship for training or research purposes.\n\nThis is where the course ends up back at its opening. The rule that governs how you source teaching material is the same rule that governs how your vendors source theirs.\n\nClosing frame for the course\n\nTwo things worth leaving them with.\n\nThe enumerated lists in Chapter 135-7 are floors. Every rule says so in its own text, and the statute at 43-10A-17(a)(6) reaches conduct causing no injury, unrelated to practice, measured against a standard it never defines.\n\nAnd the record of your reasoning is the only artifact that travels with you. Not the decision. The record.\n\nThen ninety seconds of housekeeping people thank you for. Under 135-9-.01(4)(a) they must retain CE documentation for four years and the Board keeps no files for them. Under (4)(b) false attestation on renewal is disciplinable including revocation. Under (3)(b) and (c) hours disqualified in an audit must be cured within six months or the license may face discipline.\n\n*Case facts are composite and fictional. No identifying details of any actual client, supervisee, or practice are used, consistent with 135-7-.03(2)(e). Disciplinary fact patterns are drawn from public consent agreements and orders of the Louisiana Licensed Professional Counselors Board of Examiners, 2010–2026, with names, license numbers, and employers removed. Statutory and regulatory summaries are for instructional purposes and are not legal advice; verify current text at rules.sos.ga.gov and in the O.C.G.A. before delivery.*"
